+/* **This table is not floats; it is `MnInfoDataLayout`, and most of it is
+ * strings.** The decompilation types it `AnimLoopSettings[0x12]` because that
+ * is what the first twelve bytes are, and renders the rest as float literals
+ * -- `2.405757e8f` and friends -- which on the console are simply the bytes
+ * `4D 65 6E 4D`, i.e. `"MenM"`.
+ *
+ * A float literal is a *value*, and a big-endian machine stores that value's
+ * bytes in the order that spells the string. A little-endian one stores them
+ * backwards, so every four characters come out reversed:
+ * `MenMainConCo_Top_joint` became `MneMniaoCnopoT_ioj`. `mnInfo_80252758`
+ * looks those names up with `lbArchive_LoadSections`, found nothing, left
+ * `mnInfo_804A0958.joint` NULL, and the screen's GObj got no `hsd_obj` -- so
+ * `mnInfo_802522B8` read `jobj->aobj` off NULL and faulted at **0x7c**, which
+ * is exactly `offsetof(HSD_JObj, aobj)` (P-827).
+ *
+ * The layout also runs 0xE0 bytes while the array is 0xD8, the last name
+ * spilling into `gap_07_803EFCE0_data` -- another reason to state it as one
+ * object rather than two adjacent ones.
+ *
+ * Every value below was read back out of the compiled binary and reversed,
+ * not guessed: see the P-827 row for the dump. */
+static MnInfoDataLayout mnInfo_803EFC08_layout = {
+    { 0.0f, 199.0f, 0.0f },
+    { 0x505, 0x506, 0x507, 0x508 },
